INT. CONVALESCENT HOME SOLARIUM - AFTERNOON

A glass-walled room full of wicker chairs and ferns. Pale sun. Somewhere down the corridor, a gramophone plays a waltz.

DR. HORATIO PEMBERTON-LAW, 50s, in a rumpled tweed jacket, stands by the window with his hat in his hand.

MRS. EUPHEMIA STANHOPE-ADEYEMI, 80s, in a shawl and a wheelchair, faces the garden. Her hands rest on a blanket.

HORATIO
Good afternoon, Mrs. Stanhope-Adeyemi.

EUPHEMIA
Good afternoon, Doctor. You are late.

HORATIO
The train was held up.

EUPHEMIA
Trains always are.

He pulls up a wicker chair. Sits. Opens his bag. Closes it again without taking anything out.

HORATIO
You are looking well.

EUPHEMIA
I am looking at the roses.

HORATIO
They are fine roses.

EUPHEMIA
They are blighted. The gardener lies.

HORATIO
Perhaps he is hopeful.

EUPHEMIA
Perhaps he is paid by the hour.

Horatio smiles. Smooths his hat. A nurse's shoe squeaks past in the corridor and fades.

HORATIO
I have the results.

EUPHEMIA
I supposed you might.

HORATIO
They are not what we hoped.

EUPHEMIA
(a pause)
Are they ever?

He takes a sheet of paper from his jacket. Offers it. She doesn't take it. Instead she reaches out and presses his hand, paper and all, down onto the blanket.

EUPHEMIA (CONT'D)
Read it to the roses, Doctor. They've heard worse.

HORATIO
Mrs. Stanhope-Adeyemi--

EUPHEMIA
Euphemia. I am too old to be called anything else by a man I like.

He sets the paper on his knee. Does not read it.

HORATIO
I should like to come on Thursdays.

EUPHEMIA
You come on Mondays.

HORATIO
Then Thursdays also.

EUPHEMIA
I shall tell the gardener.

HORATIO
About the Thursdays?

EUPHEMIA
About the roses. He ought to be told.

They both go still. Horatio looks at the paper; Euphemia looks at the glass. Neither looks at the other.

Down the corridor the waltz ends. The gramophone needle hisses in the empty groove. Euphemia lifts one finger, listening.

EUPHEMIA (CONT'D)
They always forget to turn it over.

Horatio stands, crosses to the door, and calls softly down the hall.

HORATIO
(calling)
Would someone turn the record, please?

He returns to the chair. A moment later the music resumes, soft and a little slow.
